speech.wav -- six seconds of a human voice, for tests/denoise.py

A noise suppressor's whole job is to leave speech alone, and no synthetic
signal stands in for a voice, so the test needs a real one.

Source:   "Autumn Prophecy", read for LibriVox's Short Poetry Collection 137
          https://archive.org/details/spc137_1411_librivox
          file spc137_autumnprophecy_ik_64kb.mp3
          (sha256 515a2a59a47f9840685ec91c647a4d2ad6e6d51e211055c0c989b2a5f4093baa)
Licence:  Public Domain Mark 1.0, as LibriVox releases every recording --
          https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/ -- so it may be
          copied, cut and redistributed without conditions.
Made by:  ffmpeg -i spc137_autumnprophecy_ik_64kb.mp3 -ss 14 -t 6 \
                 -ac 1 -ar 48000 -sample_fmt s16 speech.wav

Mono, 48 kHz, 16-bit PCM: Python's wave module reads it, so the test needs
nothing else. Speech at -18..-40 dBFS with natural pauses, in which the
recording's own room noise sits at about -60..-68 dBFS.
